.TH TUNNELSNOOP 1 "August 2026" "tunnelsnoop 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tunnelsnoop \- zero-privilege port-forward monitor and idle reaper
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tunnelsnoop
[\fB\-interval\fR \fIduration\fR]
[\fB\-kill\-idle\fR \fIduration\fR]
[\fB\-json\fR]
[\fB\-once\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tunnelsnoop
is an unprivileged Linux utility that monitors local port-forward tunnels
established by tools such as
.BR kubectl (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR cloudflared (1),
and
.BR ngrok (1).
.PP
It audits local network exposure by flagging non-loopback wildcard bindings
(e.g., \fB0.0.0.0\fR or \fB::\fR), tracks client TCP connections and process
I/O byte counters via \fI/proc\fR, and optionally terminates idle tunnels after
a specified inactivity period.
.PP
Operation requires no root privileges; it inspects only processes owned by the
invoking user's UID.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-interval " duration"
Set polling interval for continuous monitor mode (default: \fB5s\fR).
Valid units include \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, and \fBh\fR (e.g., \fB2s\fR, \fB10s\fR).
.TP
.BI \-kill\-idle " duration"
Automatically terminate any tracked tunnel whose idle duration exceeds
\fIduration\fR (e.g., \fB15m\fR, \fB1h\fR).
Defaults to \fB0\fR (reaping disabled).
Termination uses \fBSIGTERM\fR with a 5-second grace period before escalating to \fBSIGKILL\fR.
.TP
.B \-json
Emit streaming structured JSON output instead of the interactive ANSI terminal dashboard.
.TP
.B \-once
Perform a single discovery pass and exit immediately without clearing the screen.
Useful for login profiles, CI pipelines, and audit scripts.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.TP
.B Wildcard Exposure (0.0.0.0)
Tunnels bound to \fB0.0.0.0\fR or \fB::\fR listen on all local network interfaces,
exposing forwarded services to other devices on the same LAN or Wi-Fi network.
.B tunnelsnoop
highlights these with a prominent \fB[EXPOSED 0.0.0.0]\fR indicator.
.TP
.B PID Recycling Protection
Before signaling an idle process,
.B tunnelsnoop
re-verifies that \fI/proc/<pid>/comm\fR matches the expected binary name and
that \fI/proc/<pid>/fd\fR still holds the exact listening socket inode recorded
during discovery. If either check fails, termination is aborted to prevent
signaling recycled PIDs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Monitor tunnels interactively:
.IP
.B tunnelsnoop
.PP
Run a one-time audit and exit:
.IP
.B tunnelsnoop \-once
.PP
Continuously reap tunnels idle for longer than 15 minutes:
.IP
.B tunnelsnoop \-kill\-idle 15m
.PP
Emit JSON output and filter exposed tunnels with
.BR jq (1):
.IP
.B tunnelsnoop \-once \-json | jq '.[] | select(.is_wildcard)'
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/net/tcp, /proc/net/tcp6
Kernel socket tables parsed for listening and established sockets.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/comm, /proc/<pid>/cmdline
Process identity metadata.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/fd/*
File descriptor symlinks used to correlate socket inodes with processes.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/io
Process-level I/O accounting used for idle duration calculations.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kubectl-port-forward (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR proc (5)
